a.background_notes[0]={
 id:"BG001",kind:"mathematical",at:"/inference/argument/0/text",
 fact:"For c=sqrt(2*pi)>0, the Fourier-defined spaces Hsrc^s and H^s are the same periodic distributions and their norms satisfy ||h||_(Hsrc^s)=c||h||_(H^s) for each real s. The identity on polynomials induces the identity of their completions. It preserves reality and zero mean and scales time-supremum and distributional derivative norms by c.",
 grounds:[
 {location:"/definitions/2/definition",reason:"D003 defines H^s by the weighted Fourier norm and its completion, with distributional interpretation."},
 {location:"/definitions/11/definition",reason:"D024 uses exactly the same Fourier coefficients with squared norm multiplied by 2*pi and the same distributional interpretation."},
 {location:"/definitions/3/definition",reason:"D004 gives the normalized L2 and time conventions."},
 {location:"/definitions/12/definition",reason:"D025 gives L2src=Hsrc^0 with the identical time coordinate and representative conventions."}
 ],
 justification:"The displayed defining sums differ by 2*pi, so their finiteness is equivalent and square roots give the norm identity. Scaling all distances by c>0 preserves Cauchy sequences and zero-distance equivalence; applying the identity in both directions yields inverse maps of the completions. Fourier coefficient evaluation is continuous for each fixed k in either weighted norm, so these maps preserve coefficients and their defined distributional realizations. Mean zero and reality are coefficient conditions, hence preserved. Scalar scaling commutes with suprema, essential suprema and sums of norms; the identity does not change the distributional time derivative."
};
